# Containers and CI

- Target Linux Mint developer machines and one Linux VPS; use reproducible Docker Compose with separate app, worker and PostgreSQL processes.
- Pin major image and package versions and use lockfiles; do not use `latest` in production. Keep secrets out of images, git history and build logs.
- Bind developer database and admin interfaces to `127.0.0.1`; never expose PostgreSQL directly on the public interface. Public ingress through HTTPS reverse proxy.
- Use least-privileged containers, healthchecks, shutdown/restart policies and explicit persistent volumes. Do not mount host Docker socket or arbitrary host directories into app containers.
- CI must run lint, typecheck, unit/integration tests and build before staging deployment. Production deploy only with operator approval, backup/restore plan and smoke test.
- On Linux Mint Docker packages may require the Ubuntu base codename (`UBUNTU_CODENAME`); don't run OS package removal or `sudo` without approval.
